  <theorem>
    <title>3.2.14 Angle-Angle-Angle (AAA) Similarity</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Two triangles are similar if and only if they have three congruent
        angles.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <proof>
    <p>
      ( <m>\Rightarrow</m> ) Suppose <m>\triangle ABC</m> and
      <m>\triangle DEF</m> have three congruent angles:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\angle ABC\cong\angle DEF</m>,
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\angle BAC\cong\angle EDF</m>,
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\angle BCA\cong\angle EFD</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image source="asset-3-2.png"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      If any two corresponding sides are congruent, say
      <m>\overline{AB}\cong\overline{DE}</m>, then
      <m>\triangle ABC\cong\triangle DEF</m> by Angle-Side-Angle. Congruent
      triangles are similar since the ratio of the corresponding sides is 1. So,
      without loss of generality, suppose
      <md>
        |AB|&gt;|DE|.
      </md>
      By the Ruler Postulate, we can place a point <m>E'</m> such that
      <m>A-E'-B</m> and <m>\overline{AE'}\cong\overline{DE}</m>. We can also
      place a point <m>F'</m> on <m>\overleftrightarrow{AC}</m> such that
      <m>\overline{AF'}\cong\overline{DF}</m> and <m>F'</m> lies in the same
      half plane separated by <m>\overleftrightarrow{AE'}</m> as <m>C</m>. We
      first consider the case where <m>A-F'-C</m>.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image source="asset-3-2-2"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      By the SAS Postulate, <m>\triangle AE'F'\cong\triangle DEF</m>. Extend
      <m>\overline{E'F'}</m> to get vertical angles.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image source="asset-3-2-3"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      We see that we have two pairs of congruent alternate interior angles. By
      the Alternate Interior Angles Theorem (3.1.2),
      <m>\overline{E'F'}\parallel\overline{BC}</m>. So, by Corollary 3.2.13,
      <m>\triangle ABC</m> is similar to <m>\triangle AE'F'</m>. But
      <m>\triangle AE'F'\cong\triangle DEF</m>. So, <m>\triangle ABC</m> is
      similar to <m>\triangle DEF</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If instead <m>A-C-F'</m>, extend <m>\overline{BC}</m> to get that
      <m>\overline{E'F'}\parallel\overline{BC}</m> by 3.1.2.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <image source="asset-3-2-4"/>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      ( <m>\Leftarrow</m> ) Suppose <m>\triangle ABC</m> and
      <m>\triangle DEF</m> are similar. Then, by definition, their corresponding
      angles are congruent. So, they share 3 congruent angles.
    </p>
  </proof>
</article>
</pretext>